\section{Addons beyond Stata, models estimated with R}{

Besides \code{\link[=s.regress]{s.regress()}} and \code{\link[=s.logit]{s.logit()}}, \code{s.etable()} accepts models
estimated with R, also mixed with each other in one table. This has no
counterpart in Stata, whose \code{etable} works on Stata's own estimation
results only.
\itemize{
\item \code{lm()} and \code{glm()} are read directly (no further packages needed).
Coefficients, standard errors, t/z statistics and p-values as in
\code{summary()}, Wald confidence intervals (t distribution for \code{lm()} and
for \code{glm()} with estimated dispersion, normal distribution for binomial
and Poisson models), N, R-squared, adjusted R-squared, F, root MSE and
log likelihood for \code{lm()} (for weighted models R's \code{logLik()}, which
differs from Stata's definition), and for binomial and Poisson \code{glm()}
the log likelihood,
the likelihood-ratio chi-squared and McFadden's pseudo R-squared, as
Stata's \code{logit} and \code{poisson} report them.
\item All other model classes are read with \code{parameters::model_parameters()}
(package 'parameters' required), the source that
\code{sjPlot::tab_model()} uses as well. Every class supported there works,
e.g. \code{MASS::glm.nb()}, \code{MASS::polr()}, \code{nnet::multinom()},
\code{ordinal::clm()}, \code{lme4::lmer()}/\code{glmer()}, \code{nlme::lme()},
\code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB()}, \code{survival::coxph()}/\code{survreg()},
\code{betareg::betareg()}, \code{pscl::zeroinfl()}/\code{hurdle()},
\code{fixest::feols()}, \code{plm::plm()}, \code{estimatr::lm_robust()},
\code{survey::svyglm()}, \code{mgcv::gam()}, \code{quantreg::rq()}, \code{AER::ivreg()}
and Bayesian models of 'rstanarm' or 'brms'. Model statistics come from
'insight' and 'performance'.
\item Factor and logical regressors are shown like categorical variables of
\code{\link[=s.regress]{s.regress()}}. A heading row with one row per category and the base
category (treatment contrasts) hidden unless \code{showbase = TRUE}.
Interactions and transformations (\code{wt:hp}, \code{I(wt^2)}) keep R's names.
\item Further equations get their own block with a heading row. The
zero-inflation part of \code{pscl::zeroinfl()} or \code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB()}, the
cut points of ordinal models, the outcomes of \code{nnet::multinom()}. Their
rows are addressed in \code{rowlabels} as \code{"equation::term"}, e.g.
\code{"zero_inflated::wt"}, the heading as \code{"zero_inflated::"}.
}

Differences to \code{sjPlot::tab_model()}. Coefficients are shown on the
scale of the model (log odds, log counts) unless \code{eform = TRUE}, whereas
\code{tab_model()} exponentiates logit and count models by default.
Confidence intervals of \code{lm()}/\code{glm()} are Wald intervals (as in Stata),
whereas \code{tab_model()} uses profile-likelihood intervals for \code{glm()}. Of
mixed models only the fixed effects are shown, the variance components,
ICC and number of groups that \code{tab_model()} adds are not. The standard
errors are those the model reports by default (e.g. HC2 for
\code{estimatr::lm_robust()}).
}

\examples{
m1 <- s.regress(mtcars, mpg, wt)
m2 <- s.regress(mtcars, mpg, wt, hp, am)

# etable, estimates(m1 m2)
s.etable(m1, m2)

# own row and column labels, with spaces
s.etable("Model 1" = m1, "Model 2" = m2,
         rowlabels = c(wt = "Weight (1000 lbs)", hp = "Gross horsepower",
                    am = "Manual transmission", "_cons" = "Constant"))

# text in the top left cell, e.g. the dependent variable
s.etable("Model 1" = m1, "Model 2" = m2, firstcell = "Miles per gallon")

# own notes below the table
s.etable(m1, m2, notes = c("Standardfehler in Klammern.",
                           "* p < 0.05, ** p < 0.01, *** p < 0.001"))

# confidence intervals, more model statistics
s.etable(m1, m2, cstat = "ci", mstat = c("N", "r2", "r2_a"))

# logit models as odds ratios
l1 <- s.logit(mtcars, am, wt, nolog = TRUE)
s.etable(l1, eform = TRUE, mstat = c("N", "r2_p"))

# log odds, odds ratios and average marginal effects of one model
l1_or <- s.logit(mtcars, am, wt, nolog = TRUE, or = TRUE)
l1_ame <- s.logit(mtcars, am, wt, nolog = TRUE, ame = TRUE)
s.etable("Log-Odds" = l1, "Odds Ratios" = l1_or, "AME" = l1_ame,
         mstat = c("N", "r2_p", "r2_t"))

# models estimated with R (addon beyond Stata), mixed with s.regress()
fit_lm <- lm(mpg ~ wt + factor(cyl), data = mtcars)
fit_glm <- glm(am ~ wt, family = binomial, data = mtcars)
s.etable("OLS" = fit_lm, "s.regress" = m1, "Logit" = fit_glm,
         rowlabels = c("factor(cyl)" = "Cylinders"), mstat = c("N", "r2", "r2_p"))

# other model classes need the package 'parameters', as sjPlot::tab_model()
if (requireNamespace("parameters", quietly = TRUE) &&
    requireNamespace("MASS", quietly = TRUE)) {
  s.etable(MASS::glm.nb(carb ~ wt, data = mtcars))
}
}
